    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b6aed9d9-68e5-5898-932e-97fecd4bc2bf",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\ln\\left(9\\right)+\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right)$ as a single logarithm. Assume $q>0$ and $p>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the product rule for logarithms, combine the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(9\\right)+\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(9\\cdot q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow use the quotient rule to combine the remaining difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(9\\cdot q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(\\frac{9\\cdot q}{p}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
