    "question": {
      ":question/id": "af4496a0-7154-51f1-8acd-df7c2bee4640",
      ":question/problem": "Assume $p>0$.\n\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{e\\cdot p}{3}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the quotient rule for logarithms, separate the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(\\frac{e\\cdot p}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(e\\cdot p\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow use the product rule to expand the logarithm of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(e\\cdot p\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(e\\right)+\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\ln\\left(e\\right)=1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(e\\right)+\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right) \\\\ &=1+\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
