      ":question/id": "7adce857-91b2-549c-8c73-9e5b68d8232a",
      ":question/problem": "Assume $q>0$.\n\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{q\\cdot 2}{e}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the quotient rule for logarithms, separate the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(\\frac{q\\cdot 2}{e}\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(q\\cdot 2\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow use the product rule to expand the logarithm of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(q\\cdot 2\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(q\\right)+\\ln\\left(2\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\ln\\left(e\\right)=1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\ln\\left(q\\right)+\\ln\\left(2\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln\\left(q\\right)+\\ln\\left(2\\right)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
